{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 35, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-397b-a17b", "content": "Raymond III served as the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 and he played a pivotal role within the Kingdom of Jerusalem during a turbulent era marked by rising Islamic power under Saladin. His life story involves complex political maneuvering and military leadership that shaped the fate of the Crusader states in the Levant. Raymond inherited his title at a young age following the assassination of his father Raymond II and he spent his early years consolidating power while navigating dangerous rivalries among the Frankish nobility.\n\n*Early Life and Captivity*\nRaymond faced significant hardship early in his rule when Nur ad-Din captured him during a military campaign near Banias. He remained imprisoned in Aleppo for roughly ten years while his county was administered by regents. This long period of captivity deeply influenced his worldview and made him cautious about engaging in reckless warfare against Muslim forces. His release occurred only after a substantial ransom was paid and this event marked the beginning of his mature political career where he sought stability over expansion.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nThe count later became regent for King Baldwin IV who suffered from leprosy and could not rule without assistance. Raymond acted as a stabilizing force during this time yet he faced intense opposition from factions led by Raynald of Châtillon and Guy of Lusignan. These rivals favored aggressive military actions that Raymond believed would doom the kingdom. He advocated for diplomacy and truces with Saladin because he understood the fragile state of Christian defenses. His influence waned when Baldwin IV died and the crown passed to Baldwin V and then to Guy of Lusignan whose poor decisions led to disaster.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Legacy*\nRaymond commanded a large contingent of troops at the catastrophic Battle of Hattin in 1187 where the Crusader army was annihilated by Saladin. Some contemporaries accused him of treason due to his tactical retreat during the battle but modern historians often view his actions as a desperate attempt to save what forces he could. He escaped the field while many others were killed or captured and this survival allowed him to continue defending Tripoli for a short time longer. Raymond died later that same year reportedly from pleurisy and his death removed one of the most experienced leaders from the Christian side just as Saladin began conquering coastal cities. His legacy remains that of a pragmatic statesman who recognized the limits of Crusader power in the face of a unified Muslim enemy.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -8.463895e-06}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " served", "logprob": -0.001594443}, {"token": " as", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " the", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Count", "logprob": -2.384186e-07}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " from", "logprob": -0.003946512}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -0.0011298481}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -1.9073505e-06}, {"token": "2", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " until", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " his", "logprob": -9.310679e-05}, {"token": " death", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " in", "logprob": -1.9073505e-06}, {"token": " ", "logprob": -1.9073505e-06}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -7.15256e-07}, {"token": "8", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "7", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " and", "logprob": -3.4570753e-06}, {"token": " he", "logprob": -0.024682691}, {"token": " played", "logprob": -0.0061515444}, {"token": " a", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " pivotal", "logprob": -0.0030754963}, {"token": " role", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " within", "logprob": -0.03955008}, {"token